WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.270
Dr. Jack Bright’s life has been largely
defined by one really bad day.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:11.360
Namely, back when he was a mere junior researcher
from an illustrious, complicated, and Foundation-connected

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.660
family, he was given the task of carrying
the SCP-963 medallion all the way from the

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.820
test chamber back to its containment area.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.419
Simple enough, seeing as - other than being
indestructible - the medallion had no obvious

00:00:24.419 --> 00:00:26.110
anomalous effects.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:31.460
That was, until Able breached containment
nearby, and hacked the young Jack Bright to

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.350
death with his anomalous blades.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:38.990
But, in what you could either describe as
a miracle or a terrible, terrible curse, Dr.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:44.490
Bright wasn’t dead - His soul was trapped
in the medallion, taking over any body it

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:45.490
touched.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:50.620
This has given Dr. Bright a form of body-swapping
immortality, which has made him both a valuable

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:56.350
asset to the goals of the SCP Foundation and
also an extremely unhappy man.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.400
But what would you do if you were immortal
like Jack Bright?

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.870
We posed this very question in our community
posts section, and now, we’re going to see

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:10.670
what the fans of SCP Explained would do with
the poisoned gift of true immortality.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.270
The unknown avenger said, “I would become
a vigilante who doesn’t have to worry about

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.440
facing justice through the death sentence.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.850
If I get executed I’ll simply reincarnate
as someone new and continue my vigilante justice

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:23.850
work.”

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.610
Well, that’s somewhat terrifying, thank
you for sharing, unknown avenger.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:33.090
All we ask is that you politely keep your
dangerous vigilante activities away from us,

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.800
just so we don’t get caught in the crossfire.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.930
We’re really not sure the universe can take
two Deadpools…

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:43.391
Vance Alexander said, “Bearing in mind the
duplicating ability of the medallion, I'd

00:01:43.391 --> 00:01:45.690
make two of myself and then fight myself.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:50.330
Or I wouldn't limit it to two, I'd make a
whole bunch of copies and have a battle royale

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:51.330
of myself.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.270
May the best copy win!”

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:58.220
This gives us an important opportunity to
remind you of one of Dr. Bright’s lesser-known

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.690
abilities: Contact with the necklace means
immediate brain death, and if the necklace

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:07.060
is removed from a wearer, the body will simply
collapse and die.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.150
However, if the necklace is left on the body
for thirty days, a copy of Dr. Bright’s

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.379
consciousness will remain even when the necklace
is removed.

00:02:15.379 --> 00:02:18.719
Theoretically, this could be used to create
a clone army.

00:02:18.719 --> 00:02:23.769
And using that army for self-against-self
gladiator battles would undeniably be pretty

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:25.129
kickass.

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:29.010
DeadlyVeggie said, “If I was immortal, I
would try to play and complete the stories

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.430
of every D&amp;D character I have made.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.450
Now THAT would be something only an immortal
could do.”

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.760
A truly avant-garde approach: Using one infinite
life to live the countless finite lives of

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.910
others, knowing not even a Nat 20 will save
you from your own purgatorial existence.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.980
Phantom said, “That's actually a really
tough one...

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:52.439
I'm unironically scared to death of dying,
so I guess I'd just try to do the things I

00:02:52.439 --> 00:02:53.599
want to do?

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:58.219
Probably would end up trying to become a well-known
gamer or something lol, or maybe teach forever.

00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:01.139
It's depressing, since it's such a curse to
be immortal.”

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:06.650
While we do think being a teacher or a well-known
gamer are both noble pursuits for the immortal-about-town,

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:11.629
we’re honestly more curious as to what being
ironically scared of dying would be.

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:14.069
Is that what the kids are into nowadays?

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:15.909
Let us know down in the comments.

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:20.020
Mr. Knight The Detective said, “I would
have time to watch pretty much every cartoon,

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.870
anime, movie, and series I ever missed.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:26.919
After I'm done with all of them I will fulfill
my bucket list with other wishes.”

00:03:26.919 --> 00:03:30.609
This is certainly a novel way to use total
immortality.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:34.159
But a word to the wise: Make sure you get
through your watch list before the rest of

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:38.709
humanity is wiped out by whatever XK-Class
End of the World Scenario lady luck has lined

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:43.489
up for us - our money is either on climate
change or the duplicating cakes - because

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:47.269
if you’re the only one left and you happen
to break your glasses, you’ll be forever

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:50.950
wandering around the empty wastes, tearfully
murmuring, “It isn’t fair.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.730
There was time now.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:53.760
There was time…”

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.650
Whatisamelon said, “Personally, I would
try and make a time machine to watch many

00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.959
historical events unfold.

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:03.690
I mean you have plenty of time and you can’t
die from any certain event.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:04.999
It could be fun.”

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:06.269
A lofty goal indeed.

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:10.360
Here’s a fun exercise for you, to immediately
find out if ever you’re going to create

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:15.040
or at least obtain time travel technology
in your lifetime: Make a promise to yourself

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.209
that if you do, you’ll come back to this
exact moment.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:19.209
Okay.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:20.209
Good.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:24.050
However, if a version of you from the future
hasn’t just appeared in front of you, then

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:29.490
I’m afraid I have some very disappointing
news for you on the whole time travel thing…

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:33.220
Cade Peterson said, “I am kinda confused
with how the amulet works.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:38.620
If dr bright died, and then somebody put the
amulet on an animal, would he become the animal,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:42.379
and still be able to talk, or would the animal
just turn in to him.

00:04:42.379 --> 00:04:46.550
Next question with that in mind, couldnt he
just get it to be put on an anomolous object,

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:49.160
such as 682, and use it for good?”

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.650
All very valid questions that is worth answering
before we move on.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:57.449
First, if Dr. Bright’s 963 amulet was placed
on the body of an animal, it would merely

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:03.200
plant his consciousness into the animal - not
in any way physically transform the beast.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.100
Whether the animal could talk purely depends
on whether it has the necessary anatomy to

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.879
imitate human-esque speech patterns.

00:05:09.879 --> 00:05:15.179
And as to your second question, this has been
attempted, with SCP-682 specifically, in fact

00:05:15.179 --> 00:05:18.970
- see our previous video on how that epic
battle played out.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:24.870
But to give you an abridged version, some
anomalous entities are more resistant to SCP-963’s

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.070
abilities than us mere humans are.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:30.180
Not all potential hosts are created equal.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:34.889
FoxyGamer said, “I’d drink one drop of
water from every country and one drop of lava

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:39.450
from every volcano then probably watch as
every generation of humanity expand and rule

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:40.450
it.”

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:45.169
This is the exact kind of bizarre, inexplicable
madness we were hoping to see when we first

00:05:45.169 --> 00:05:46.599
posted this question.

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:52.310
The specificity of these suggestions implies
a fascinating level of planning and foresight.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:56.300
And it’s undeniable that if you were able
to prove to people you had imbibed a drop

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:02.150
of lava from every single volcano on earth
- there’s about 1350 active ones, by the

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:09.389
way - you probably would have some claim to
being humanity’s supreme ruler.

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:14.250
Mangolad said, “If I had the power like
Bright, I would create the ultimate wine - make

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.330
it rest on a barrel as long as possible.”

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:20.849
Ah, yes, I see we have a person of culture
among us.

00:06:20.849 --> 00:06:26.710
Can’t wait for all the comments to be about
me saying “Among Us.”

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:31.169
Nonetheless, having Dr. Bright’s immortality
would be an excellent excuse to cultivate

00:06:31.169 --> 00:06:34.680
some of the finest aged wine the world has
ever known.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.949
Perhaps those sweet overtones and nutty undertones
will dull the existential horror of never

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:45.310
knowing the eternal rest of death…

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:47.310
Quote C said, “Oh boy!

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:48.310
Immortality!

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.949
Now I can dedicate more time to my favorite
hobby!

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:53.090
Procrastinating on animation projects!”

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:57.659
That’s the can-do attitude we love here
at SCP Explained!

00:06:57.659 --> 00:07:01.849
Eternity certainly lasts a while, imagine
all the incredible animations you could not

00:07:01.849 --> 00:07:03.860
get done in that time!

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:04.860
Inspirational!

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:10.249
_theoutsider_ said, “Dedicating yourself
to something meaningful like Jack Bright is

00:07:10.249 --> 00:07:11.840
the only way to do it.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:15.289
That is, of course, after you had some fun
for the first few decades.”

00:07:15.289 --> 00:07:17.770
We couldn’t agree more, outsider.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:23.240
After all, “You Only Live Once” still
applies, even if that Once lasts forever.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:27.560
Dr. Bright tried to have some fun of his own
before he leveled out, and now there’s a

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:31.039
very long list of things he’s not allowed
to do.

00:07:31.039 --> 00:07:33.030
Perhaps we should cover that someday…

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:37.180
Michael Awesome said, “If I were immortal,
I would steer humanity into the direction

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:41.930
of logic and science, guiding them from the
shadows into an eternal golden age using the

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:44.889
resources I have accumulated through forever.”

00:07:44.889 --> 00:07:47.669
That certainly sounds Awesome, Michael.

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:53.060
However, feel free to call us pessimists,
but humanity has never been that easy to steer

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:54.190
out of the shadows.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:59.939
See: The long list of very smart, paradigm-shifting
individuals executed as witches or heretics

00:07:59.939 --> 00:08:05.069
for such bold and radical claims as “the
earth rotates around the sun” and “math

00:08:05.069 --> 00:08:06.069
exists.”

00:08:06.069 --> 00:08:09.610
But we’ll be happy to be proven wrong on
this front, of course.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:15.350
Silent Monke said, “Dedicate my life into
turning into a hotdog sandwich.”

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:21.580
Hey, if you can find a very unscrupulous man
with a meat-mincing machine, you may take

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:27.930
a lot less time than eternity to see this
peculiar dream become a glorious reality.

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:33.740
Noweq 69 - nice - said, “I would make an
army of myself and when i wouldn't want to

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:37.290
go the store then i would just send my clone
for some doritos.”

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:42.330
There are a lot of truly nefarious and megalomaniacal
things you can do with an army of like-minded

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:47.590
clones - like the battle royale guy from earlier
- so it’s relieving that you’d just use

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:50.200
yours for performing menial tasks.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:54.160
But bear in mind, just because these clones
are like-minded doesn’t mean they’ll be

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:55.710
subservient to you.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:09:00.630
If you’re the controlling type, you might
even have a violent clone uprising on your

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:04.530
hands, and trust us, nobody likes an Attack
of the Clones.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:05.580
Food for thought…

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:10.370
Thevoicesgaming said, “Depending on how
the medallion works I’d try to keep me and

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:15.850
the women I love alive if I can’t do that
then I’d give up the medallion to the Scp

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:20.830
foundation (assuming it exists) a life without
someone isn’t a life worth living no matter

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:24.270
how much you’ve accomplished it’ll always
get boring eventually.”

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:25.880
A very poignant point.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:28.570
We have good and bad news.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:33.320
Technically speaking, with the help of your
963 medallion, your loved one’s body can

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:34.320
survive.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:38.040
However, their brain will technically be occupied
by a copy of your own consciousness.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.950
But if that copy is willing to play pretend,
well, maybe you can make some kind of mutually-beneficial

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:44.950
arrangement?

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:48.900
The finer details of that will be something
for you and yourself to sort out.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:49.900
Good luck.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:54.660
FoxFilms said, “I would freak someone out
by repeatedly (over the course of a few years)

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:59.320
going up to them in different bodies, and
saying the same phrase each time.”

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:03.340
Using your abilities to gaslight one individual
over the course of several years definitely

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:07.960
seems like something Dr. Bright might do if
he was in a bad mood, so we’re sure he would

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:11.530
appreciate this act of extreme anomalous pettiness.

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:15.960
Just promise you won’t do it to us - This
really isn’t something we want on our plate

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:16.960
right now.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:21.480
Zero Confidence said, “Well, a good portion
of my lives would be developing a meaningful

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:26.550
relationship with 682 via the process of annoying
them with my presence every few weeks, or

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:30.660
when I’m just tired of my current body and
ready to move on to that nice D-class who

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:31.820
was next in line.”

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:35.600
This is definitely an original one.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.350
We appreciate that you’re still rosy-eyed
about the possibility of a single atom of

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:44.410
goodness residing somewhere deep beneath SCP-682’s
nasty, scaly hide.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:49.390
Whether you’ll ever actually find it - even
with an eternity of searching - is another

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:50.390
question.

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:54.380
But if Good Will Hunting has taught us anything,
if you repeat “It’s Not Your Fault”

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:59.120
enough, even if it kills you a thousand times
in between, it might eventually have a cathartic

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:00.200
emotional outpouring.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.790
Hey, it’s worth a try, right?

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:08.440
Cracker County said, “I'd enact minuscule
amounts of tomfoolery at the expense of others.”

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:12.440
Hey, you know what you like, and you’re
doing what you can to obtain it.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.690
We can’t help but respect that about you.

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:19.840
Sometimes, setting modest yet attainable goals
is the most efficient way to go, even when

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:21.940
you have eternity at your fingertips.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:27.100
Nicholas Lau said, “Personally, I'd go into
space with a lot of pain medication and magnetic

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:29.620
boots, then do laps around the asteroid belt.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:33.950
If I'm immortal, even with all that pain,
I could do a proper jog that isn't just strolling

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:34.950
around the block.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:41.110
Also, figure out a cure for cancer, and whipping
SCP-038 with really long spaghetti for infinite

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:42.110
food.”

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:43.110
Wow.

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:47.480
This is a hell of a bucket list, even for
someone who knows they’re never actually

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:48.570
going to kick that bucket.

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:52.680
We don’t want to mess with your dreams here,
but it is important to remember that there

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:57.750
is considerable distance between asteroids
in an asteroid belt, even in a low-gravity

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:01.840
environment, so you may need to seriously
work on your jumping skills.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:05.120
The cure for cancer is an undeniably noble
goal.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:10.660
And we’re personally very invested in you
using SCP-038 - A tree that clones anything

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.630
that touches it - to create infinite spaghetti.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:16.040
We find that idea to be molto bene.

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:20.690
Minescarfedbro said, “If I was immortal
I would probably go to the cell of "shy guy"

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:22.010
and look at him.”

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:27.010
That is certainly an interesting way to spend
your time, but there are two worrying potential

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:28.870
issues that could come up here.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:34.810
Famously, SCP-096 leaves no trace of its victims,
so it’d be fascinating to see if the SCP-963

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.520
medallion would be left after the onslaught.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:41.971
However, we find our second concern to be
an even more frightening possibility: What

00:12:41.971 --> 00:12:46.830
if the rage state of SCP-096 carried over
between bodies?

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:53.790
What if, every time you regenerated, SCP-096
was activated anew, and made a beeline for

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:54.790
you?

00:12:54.790 --> 00:13:00.760
You would be in the worst kind of hell: Caught
in an infinite loop of being murdered by SCP-096

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:05.970
again and again, until the world collapses
between the two of you.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:11.880
So yes, we personally think that this little
stunt just may not be worth the risk…

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:16.540
Darfordial said, “I would probably spend
my time learning all languages, science, and

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:20.880
basically everything I’d guess, and then
try to ascend high in the ranks of the foundation

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:25.930
and someday become a member of the O5 Council
(unlikely though since the O5 could be immortal

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:30.890
as well) and maybe accumulate more power by
throwing my medallion at anomalies.”

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:35.950
Funnily enough, this is pretty much exactly
how Jack Bright himself uses the medallion,

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:40.450
so you’d probably be in good company with
the SCP Foundation’s grumpiest immortal!

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:45.060
Pandie Goodies said, “I'd probably do whatever
I can to ensure every meme and joke thrown

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:46.440
at the internet revivable.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:50.350
I'll bring back cat burgers memes in the year
3022.”

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:55.090
Of all of the suggestions we’ve observed
today, this is undeniably the evilest of them

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:56.310
all, Pandie Goodies.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:14:01.420
And we swear that if you use this power to
give immortality to the Among Us memes that

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:06.820
plague our comment sections, you will have
made a powerful enemy in SCP Explained today,

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:10.070
you have our word on that.

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:15.370
Samantha Agius said, “To be honest I feel
sorry for Dr. Jack Bright and his family(everyone

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:16.990
deserves a fresh start right?)”

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:18.900
We’re with you on this one, Samantha.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:22.810
The ballad of Jack Bright himself, and the
terrible tragedies and upsets that pervade

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:25.290
his family, easily tugs at the heartstrings.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:31.870
If today has taught us anything, it’s that
there truly is no longevity without tragedy.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:35.820
Horseman Gaming said, “If I was immortal,
I'd spend my time finding ways to become as

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:41.180
smart as Rick Sanchez, invent the interdimensional
portal gun, and start dimension hopping...or,

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:45.850
more accurately, pitching my medallion into
the portal, seeing where it goes.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:47.820
After all, it is indestructible.”

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:53.540
Using the SCP-963 medallion to become Rick
Sanchez is certainly a new one.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:56.830
Perhaps then you can place the medallion onto
a pickle, and it’ll be the funniest--

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:59.040
Wait, what do you mean it’s a dead meme?

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:02.600
Well, it doesn’t matter, because if Pandie
Goodies gets their way, no meme will ever

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:07.010
really die, no matter how obnoxious!

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:11.550
Prisimity said, “I would plant as many trees
in the world as I can and watch them grow.”

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:15.990
Well, that’s just incredibly wholesome,
and we think this would be a truly wonderful

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:18.010
way to use the power of immortality.

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:21.630
A shiny SCP Foundation gold star for you,
Prisimity!

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:26.250
UAF Gaming said, “I'd probably actually
start working on my mental health.”

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.800
Hey, for what it’s worth, don’t wait for
the gift of immortality to be bestowed on

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:32.720
you before you start working on your mental
health.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:36.420
Working on yourself is always a good thing,
and if you’ve been looking for an excuse,

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.590
there’s no better time to start than right
now.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:44.170
Leora Sellouk said, “After sulking over
how everyone I know and will know is either

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:48.960
dead or will die during my never-ending lifespan,
I would learn everything I could about any

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:54.450
topic simply because I had the time to, whether
it be about the weather or about anomalies

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:55.970
and the foundation.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:16:00.970
I would set to working my way into the foundation
and getting a good acceptable job that gave

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:05.250
me enough clearance to be able to access the
most information possible.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:10.370
I would then work in two ways, one by helping
with the actual goals of the foundation and

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:15.840
all that comes with that, and also by working
my passion, writing stories, and implementing

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:20.470
the many things I would see working at such
a place as the SCP foundation, different enough

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:24.750
that it wasn’t an exact copy, but similar
enough so that if someone were to read it

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:28.990
after having heard rumors about such a beast,
they would assume it came from the book and

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:34.590
was in fact, fiction and not reality, an extra
defense against people finding out the truth.”

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:35.590
Dr. Bright…

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:36.590
Is that you?

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:40.860
What did we tell you about making sock puppet
accounts and commenting on our community posts?

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:45.150
The O5 Council is going to give you a very
stern talking to about this…

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:50.580
Jhonnyplays said, “I’d play every video
game out there and I’d watch every SCP Explained

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.030
- Story &amp; Animation video.”

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:58.420
We think this is a truly excellent idea, and
something we should all do right now, even

00:16:58.420 --> 00:16:59.920
if we’re not immortal.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:04.569
Heck, perhaps you should start with “SCP
Immortal Dr. Bright Explained” and “DR.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:10.829
BRIGHT FOR PRESIDENT SCP-4444 - Bush v. Gore”,
a great pair of videos if you’re interested

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:14.850
in more exploits from the truly legendary
Dr. Jack Bright!

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:15.850
What?

00:17:15.850 --> 00:17:16.530
We’re just saying…

